---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Neo4j Stock Network Analysis"
  eyebrow="GRAPH ANALYTICS"
  description="Representing historical stock-return relationships as a graph and testing whether sophisticated centrality measures materially change what simpler network measures reveal."
  heroImage="/images/projects/neo4j-correlation-network.png"
  heroAlt="NASDAQ stock correlation network with connected and isolated stocks"
  tools="Neo4j · Cypher · Graph Data Science · Python"
  metrics={[
    { value: '85', label: 'stocks' },
    { value: '197', label: 'relationships at 0.55' },
    { value: '39', label: 'isolated stocks' },
    { value: '4', label: 'thresholds tested' }
  ]}
  links={[
    {
      label: 'Interactive Network',
      href: '/interactive/neo4j-stock-network.html'
    },
    {
      label: 'View GitHub',
      href: 'https://github.com/Ryan-Wenstrom-Portfolio/neo4j-stock-network-analysis'
    }
  ]}
>
  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Problem</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>
        A correlation matrix can show pairwise relationships, but it is harder
        to reason about structural roles, connected components, central stocks,
        and isolated stocks from a table alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        This project turns historical return correlations into a graph,
        compares degree and PageRank-based centrality, and tests how much the
        conclusions depend on the correlation threshold used to construct the
        network.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Graph construction</h2>
    <div class="pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>01</span><strong>Adjusted close prices</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>02</span><strong>Coverage filtering</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>03</span><strong>Daily log returns</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>04</span><strong>Pearson correlations</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>05</span><strong>Neo4j network</strong></div>
      <div class="pipeline-step"><span>06</span><strong>GDS centrality + sensitivity</strong></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Main result</h2>
    <div class="finding-grid">
      <div class="finding">
        <strong>0.971</strong>
        <span>Correlation between degree and unweighted PageRank.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <strong>0.970</strong>
        <span>Correlation between degree and weighted PageRank.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <strong>0.999</strong>
        <span>Correlation between weighted and unweighted PageRank.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <strong>46-stock core</strong>
        <span>Connected core at the selected 0.55 correlation threshold.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Centrality comparison</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <p>
        In this constructed network, PageRank adds limited incremental
        information beyond simple connectivity. The result is analytically
        useful because it shows when a more sophisticated graph measure
        confirms, rather than overturns, a simpler structural measure.
      </p>

      <figure class="supporting-visual">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/neo4j-degree-vs-pagerank.png"
          alt="Degree versus PageRank comparison chart"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Degree and weighted PageRank move closely together in the selected
          network.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Threshold sensitivity</h2>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="finding-grid">
        <div class="finding"><strong>0.50</strong><span>430 edges · 27 isolates</span></div>
        <div class="finding"><strong>0.55</strong><span>197 edges · 39 isolates</span></div>
        <div class="finding"><strong>0.60</strong><span>98 edges · 49 isolates</span></div>
        <div class="finding"><strong>0.65</strong><span>42 edges · 58 isolates</span></div>
      </div>

      <figure class="supporting-visual">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/neo4j-threshold-sensitivity.png"
          alt="Neo4j network threshold sensitivity chart"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Network density and isolation change materially as the correlation
          threshold becomes more restrictive.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <a
          class="inline-link"
          href="/interactive/neo4j-stock-network.html"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Open the interactive network →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Historical return correlation is not causality, investment quality, or
        a prediction of future co-movement. Network structure is conditional on
        the selected construction threshold, so centrality conclusions should
        be interpreted as properties of this historical graph rather than
        trading signals.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
